<h2>Step 1: Send your requirement</h2>
<p>Every order starts the same way: you tell us the product, quantity, destination and any specification or packaging requirement you already know. Use the <a href="/get-a-quote/">quote form</a> or WhatsApp — either works, and the form is faster if you already know your specification, since it prompts for the details we'll need anyway.</p>

<h2>Step 2: We confirm specifications</h2>
<p>Within 24 business hours, we come back with what's currently available at your specification, or ask clarifying questions if your requirement needs more detail — grade, size, moisture, packaging format, whatever applies to the product. This is the step most likely to slow down if your initial enquiry is vague ("good quality rice" vs. "1121 basmati, sella, 2% broken, 12% moisture") — the more specific you are upfront, the faster this moves.</p>

<h2>Step 3: You receive a quote</h2>
<p>An FOB, CIF, CFR or EXW price for your quantity, with a stated validity window. Commodity and freight prices move, so quotes aren't open-ended — if you don't order within the window, ask for a current quote rather than assuming the old number still holds.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Samples, if you need them</h2>
<p>For a first order at meaningful volume, requesting a sample is usually worth the time. See our <a href="/sample-policy/">sample policy</a> for cost and lead time — it varies by product and whether the sample needs special handling (frozen seafood, for example).</p>

<h2>Step 5: Contract terms agreed</h2>
<p>Specification, quantity, packaging, Incoterm, payment terms and delivery window all go into writing. This is the point where "we'll sort out details later" stops being acceptable — vague contracts are where disputes come from later.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Payment terms confirmed</h2>
<p>Letter of Credit, advance payment/Cash Against Documents, or Telegraphic Transfer — see <a href="/payment-terms/">Payment Terms</a> for how we decide which fits your order.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Sourcing, production and packing</h2>
<p>Product is sourced or produced, graded and packed to the agreed specification. For fresh produce this means harvest and pre-cooling timed to your shipment; for processed goods it means production scheduling against your delivery window.</p>

<h2>Step 8: Pre-shipment inspection</h2>
<p>Third-party inspection (SGS, Bureau Veritas, Intertek or your nominated agency) can be arranged on request, or we use our standard pre-shipment check. This happens before loading, not after — the whole point is catching a problem while it's still fixable.</p>

<h2>Step 9: Documentation prepared</h2>
<p>Export documentation prepared to match destination-country and buyer requirements — phytosanitary certificate, certificate of origin, fumigation certificate, lab test reports and more, as applicable. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment before it ships, not after.</p>

<h2>Step 10: Shipping</h2>
<p>Loaded at the relevant Indian port and booked with a shipping line matched to your destination. See <a href="/logistics-shipping/">Logistics &amp; Shipping</a> for transit-time ranges by region.</p>

<h2>Step 11: Delivery</h2>
<p>Tracked through to your destination port, with documentation sent ahead so your customs clearance isn't waiting on paperwork that should already be in hand.</p>

<h2>What does a realistic timeline look like?</h2>
<p>For in-season fresh produce or already-milled/packed staples, 2-3 weeks from confirmed order to vessel departure is typical. For products that need sourcing to order, or where your specification requires production scheduling, expect longer — we'll tell you a realistic number when we quote rather than a generic estimate.</p>

<h2>What's different about a repeat order?</h2>
<p>Once specification, payment terms and documentation requirements are established, repeat orders move faster — steps 2, 5 and 6 largely become confirmations rather than fresh negotiations. This is part of why we'd rather start with an honest trial order than oversell a first shipment.</p>
